From: Ирганов А.Н.
Date: 2026-10-04
Subject: [PATCH] seL4: enable SMAP even with CONFIG_PRINTING (boot-log console)

seL4 x86_64 disables SMAP (Supervisor Mode Access Prevention) whenever
CONFIG_PRINTING is on, because the comment claims the "user stack trace" reads
user memory and SMAP would fault it. In fact Arch_userStackTrace reads user
memory via readWordFromVSpace -> paddr_to_pptr (the supervisor-mapped kernel
window), which SMAP does NOT fault.

Schild TCB forces CONFIG_PRINTING=1 for the framebuffer boot-log console, so
SMAP was silently off. This patch enables SMAP regardless of CONFIG_PRINTING,
keeping SMEP and CR0.WP; only CONFIG_DANGEROUS_CODE_INJECTION still blocks it.

diff --git a/src/arch/x86/kernel/boot.c b/src/arch/x86/kernel/boot.c
index 93b3209..fa004f6 100644
--- a/src/arch/x86/kernel/boot.c
+++ b/src/arch/x86/kernel/boot.c
@@ -373,9 +373,11 @@ BOOT_CODE bool_t init_cpu(
     cpuid_007h_ebx_t ebx_007;
     ebx_007.words[0] = x86_cpuid_ebx(0x7, 0);
     if (cpuid_007h_ebx_get_smap(ebx_007)) {
-        /* if we have user stack trace enabled or dangerous code injection then we cannot
-         * enable this as SMAP will make them fault. */
-        if (!config_set(CONFIG_PRINTING) && !config_set(CONFIG_DANGEROUS_CODE_INJECTION)) {
+        /* Schild TCB: enable SMAP even with CONFIG_PRINTING. The user stack
+         * trace (Arch_userStackTrace) reads user memory via the kernel window
+         * (readWordFromVSpace -> paddr_to_pptr), which is supervisor-mapped, so
+         * SMAP does not fault it. Only dangerous code injection still blocks. */
+        if (!config_set(CONFIG_DANGEROUS_CODE_INJECTION)) {
             write_cr4(read_cr4() | CR4_SMAP);
         }
     }
